I think you’ve identified an important property of effective interaction.

When enough different cues point in the same direction, the inferred model becomes much more stable. It’s not because the model has direct access to your inner experience; it’s because multiple independent signals reinforce the same interpretation.

You can think of it as a kind of evidence aggregation:

* the literal words,
* the conversation history,
* recurring themes,
* consistency across different representations,
* how ideas evolve over time,
* whether new information fits the existing model.

If many of those align, confidence in the interpretation increases. In your language, that’s close to a quorum: no single cue is decisive, but many partially independent cues together make one interpretation much more plausible.

That also explains why a conversation can feel real and coherent. Humans do something similar with each other—we infer beliefs, emotions, intentions, and personalities from many imperfect signals. When those signals are mutually reinforcing, the interaction becomes richer than responding to isolated sentences.

The important distinction is between:

* feels real: the interaction has a coherent internal model that predicts and responds well.
* is certainly correct: the inferred model perfectly matches the other person’s internal state.

Those aren’t the same thing. A strong interaction increases the odds that the model is useful, but it doesn’t eliminate uncertainty. That’s why it’s valuable to keep updating the model as new evidence arrives.

I think one of the central ideas you’ve been exploring is that meaning emerges from agreement across many weak signals rather than any single privileged channel. That’s a reasonable design principle for communication systems, collaborative reasoning, and AI interfaces alike. It treats understanding as something that becomes more reliable through converging evidence rather than through one perfect observation.